1
00:00:00,280 --> 00:00:02,360
Welcome to Before the Merge, a series for

2
00:00:02,360 --> 00:00:04,200
small software teams evaluating coding

3
00:00:04,200 --> 00:00:04,760
agents.

4
00:00:05,080 --> 00:00:07,800
This is episode one. More agent output,

5
00:00:07,800 --> 00:00:08,640
better delivery.

6
00:00:09,240 --> 00:00:11,080
Our evidence is current as of October

7
00:00:11,080 --> 00:00:12,680
15th, 2026.

8
00:00:13,120 --> 00:00:15,240
This presentation uses an AI generated

9
00:00:15,240 --> 00:00:16,400
presenter and voice.

10
00:00:16,666 --> 00:00:18,386
The central question is not whether an

11
00:00:18,386 --> 00:00:20,786
agent can produce code. It is what a team

12
00:00:20,786 --> 00:00:22,946
should change when agents can participate

13
00:00:22,946 --> 00:00:24,866
in planning, implementation and

14
00:00:24,866 --> 00:00:25,786
verification.

15
00:00:26,186 --> 00:00:27,586
The important development in these

16
00:00:27,586 --> 00:00:29,586
sources is a more structured way of

17
00:00:29,586 --> 00:00:31,066
organizing agent work.

18
00:00:31,386 --> 00:00:34,026
A workflow demonstration, however, is not

19
00:00:34,026 --> 00:00:35,746
a measured improvement in software

20
00:00:35,746 --> 00:00:36,386
delivery.

21
00:00:37,346 --> 00:00:38,220
Our first source

22
00:00:38,220 --> 00:00:40,226
is Jake Cukjati's AIMUG presentation,

23
00:00:40,226 --> 00:00:42,226
The Coding Factory.

24
00:00:42,626 --> 00:00:44,866
Around 5 minutes and 42 seconds, he

25
00:00:44,866 --> 00:00:46,986
describes taking a specification into

26
00:00:46,986 --> 00:00:48,426
implementation planning.

27
00:00:48,786 --> 00:00:50,826
By roughly six minutes and 11 seconds,

28
00:00:51,106 --> 00:00:53,026
he says the agents test and implement the

29
00:00:53,026 --> 00:00:53,466
work.

30
00:00:53,786 --> 00:00:55,586
That is a practitioner's description of a

31
00:00:55,586 --> 00:00:56,266
process.

32
00:00:56,546 --> 00:00:58,266
It does not establish that his team

33
00:00:58,266 --> 00:00:58,411
spends

34
00:00:58,411 --> 00:01:00,586
less time reviewing or that fewer

35
00:01:00,586 --> 00:01:01,906
defects reach users.

36
00:01:02,226 --> 00:01:04,106
Those are separate outcomes requiring

37
00:01:04,106 --> 00:01:05,146
separate evidence.

38
00:01:05,946 --> 00:01:08,346
The practical distinction is important. A

39
00:01:08,346 --> 00:01:10,146
specification can make the intended

40
00:01:10,146 --> 00:01:11,146
result visible.

41
00:01:11,426 --> 00:01:13,466
A test can check some of that result.

42
00:01:13,746 --> 00:01:15,706
Neither guarantees that the specification

43
00:01:15,706 --> 00:01:17,278
is complete, or that the test

44
00:01:17,278 --> 00:01:18,826
asks the right question.

45
00:01:19,306 --> 00:01:21,146
A team still needs someone responsible

46
00:01:21,146 --> 00:01:23,066
for deciding what acceptable behavior

47
00:01:23,066 --> 00:01:25,546
means, including failure cases and the

48
00:01:25,546 --> 00:01:27,106
consequences of a mistake.

49
00:01:27,906 --> 00:01:29,786
Our second source is Salvatore Sanfilippo's

50
00:01:29,786 --> 00:01:31,746
video about his approach to

51
00:01:31,746 --> 00:01:32,906
automatic programming.

52
00:01:33,266 --> 00:01:35,386
Around 3 minutes and 3 seconds, the

53
00:01:35,386 --> 00:01:36,798
retained translated captions

54
00:01:36,798 --> 00:01:38,506
lead into a discussion of

55
00:01:38,506 --> 00:01:40,746
interacting with an agent through design,

56
00:01:40,786 --> 00:01:43,106
code, verification, and small changes.

57
00:01:43,506 --> 00:01:45,506
The contrast is with treating success as

58
00:01:45,506 --> 00:01:47,466
a matter of writing one perfect prompt.

59
00:01:47,826 --> 00:01:49,706
This is an iterative process, not a

60
00:01:49,706 --> 00:01:50,626
single hand off.

61
00:01:51,186 --> 00:01:52,866
Because our evidence uses translated

62
00:01:52,866 --> 00:01:55,226
captions, wording and interpretation

63
00:01:55,226 --> 00:01:56,506
remain a limitation.

64
00:01:56,866 --> 00:01:59,226
Our third source is The Modern SDE's

65
00:01:59,226 --> 00:02:01,106
discussion of agent categories.

66
00:02:01,586 --> 00:02:03,866
Around 37 seconds, the speaker groups

67
00:02:03,866 --> 00:02:05,546
agents according to what they do.

68
00:02:06,106 --> 00:02:08,186
We use that distinction narrowly before

69
00:02:08,186 --> 00:02:10,386
evaluating an agent to find its job.

70
00:02:10,666 --> 00:02:12,706
An assistant that suggests a change and a

71
00:02:12,706 --> 00:02:14,906
system that executes a task should not

72
00:02:14,906 --> 00:02:16,106
automatically receive the same

73
00:02:16,106 --> 00:02:18,266
permissions or be evaluated by the same

74
00:02:18,266 --> 00:02:18,906
standard.

75
00:02:19,426 --> 00:02:22,066
That comparison is our interpretation not

76
00:02:22,066 --> 00:02:23,666
a measured result from the video.

77
00:02:24,386 --> 00:02:25,986
Putting these sources together gives us a

78
00:02:25,986 --> 00:02:26,826
useful framework.

79
00:02:27,306 --> 00:02:29,346
Define the task, make the intended

80
00:02:29,346 --> 00:02:31,346
behavior explicit and preserve

81
00:02:31,346 --> 00:02:33,026
opportunities to inspect the work.

82
00:02:33,706 --> 00:02:35,146
The contribution is the connection

83
00:02:35,146 --> 00:02:37,666
between task definition, specifications

84
00:02:37,666 --> 00:02:38,666
and iteration.

85
00:02:39,066 --> 00:02:40,866
It is not proof that a particular tool

86
00:02:40,866 --> 00:02:41,005
improves

87
00:02:41,005 --> 00:02:41,866
quality.

88
00:02:42,546 --> 00:02:44,866
For example, a team might try one bounded

89
00:02:44,866 --> 00:02:45,826
maintenance task.

90
00:02:46,186 --> 00:02:47,866
Before starting, describe expected

91
00:02:47,866 --> 00:02:50,106
behavior and who will review the change.

92
00:02:50,586 --> 00:02:52,426
During the work, keep the proposed code

93
00:02:52,426 --> 00:02:53,626
and test results visible.

94
00:02:54,066 --> 00:02:55,906
Afterwards, record review time,

95
00:02:55,946 --> 00:02:58,066
corrections and unresolved risks.

96
00:02:58,746 --> 00:03:01,066
This is a proposed evaluation, not an

97
00:03:01,066 --> 00:03:02,306
experiment we conducted.

98
00:03:02,986 --> 00:03:03,275
There is a

99
00:03:03,275 --> 00:03:04,306
counterargument,

100
00:03:04,586 --> 00:03:06,626
adding checkpoints can itself consume

101
00:03:06,626 --> 00:03:06,986
time.

102
00:03:07,666 --> 00:03:09,106
That is why the next decision should be

103
00:03:09,106 --> 00:03:11,226
reversible and measured, rather than a

104
00:03:11,226 --> 00:03:12,746
blanket expansion of autonomy.

105
00:03:13,506 --> 00:03:14,253
More generated code

106
00:03:14,253 --> 00:03:16,226
is not the same as better delivery.

107
00:03:16,906 --> 00:03:18,906
These resources help frame a workflow to

108
00:03:18,906 --> 00:03:21,106
evaluate, they do not settle whether it

109
00:03:21,106 --> 00:03:21,626
pays off.

110
00:03:22,066 --> 00:03:23,946
In episode two, we examine the difference

111
00:03:23,946 --> 00:03:26,386
between a productivity claim and evidence

112
00:03:26,386 --> 00:03:27,226
that supports it.
